# 0017. The writer may edit from any message

- Status: Accepted (owner, 2026-11-25; revised after Codex's critique the same day)
- Date: 2026-11-25
- Amends: [0004](0004-cli-agent-adapters.adr) (who is writable)

Today only a message to the `writer` alone is writable; `@all`, several mentions and
`default = "all"` run every agent read-only (Claude in plan mode). With the owner's
`default = "all"`, that means nothing ever edits unless they remember `@claude`, which is
the "stuck in plan mode, can't work from rt" problem. This ADR lets the writer edit
whenever a message reaches it; everyone else stays read-only.

## Technical details
- **Rule**: a user message that reaches the `writer` gives the writer a writable turn,
  whoever else it reaches. The other recipients stay read-only (in their worktrees once
  [0016](0016-worktree-per-agent.adr) lands). Still never writable: `ask` turns
  ([0007](0007-model-to-model-communication.adr)), handoffs, and any turn while `/talk`
  or a policy routes it from another model.
- **Order inside a broadcast**: the read-only recipients start at once, as today, and
  each sees the files as they are when its own turn starts (a queued reviewer may see
  the writer's edits so far). No pre-edit snapshot is promised: a shared snapshot taken
  before the writer spawns would need one frozen mirror per broadcast, kept for queued
  recipients; not worth it until a reviewer's view of a half-done edit proves a problem.
- **Every existing writable check stays**: the edit lock (outside git, unknown checkout,
  crash marker, [0012](0012-checkout-writer-lock.adr)), the queued-turn recheck when a
  held writable turn finally starts, and the lock itself. Contention in this roundtable
  queues the writer's turn, as today; a writer running in another roundtable on the same
  checkout refuses it with that reason (0012), it does not wait.
- **Opt out per message**: start the message with `@all?` for "just discuss, nobody
  touches anything": everyone read-only, as today's broadcasts. The echo line shows the
  effective permission, after the checks above: `you → all (claude may edit)`,
  `you → all (read-only)`, or `you → all (read-only: edits locked …)`.
- **Registry**: no new key; `writer` unset still means nobody edits (today's behaviour).
- **Code**: `App::submit` decides `writable` per target instead of per message:
  `writable = target == writer && !read_only_marker && !self.locked()`; the
  `mentions > 1` read-only rule goes, nothing else. Tests: broadcast with writer (writer
  writable, others not), `@all?` (none), `default = "all"` (writer writable), writer
  disabled (none), edits locked (none, echo says why), writer busy (queued, still
  writable when it starts unless locked by then).
- Consequences: the owner's `default = "all"` setup can work without `@claude` every time.
  Costs: a broadcast now may change files while others read them, so a reviewer's answer
  can describe a half-edited tree (with 0016 it reads a mirror synced when its turn
  starts, consistent within that turn but not pre-edit), and the "every broadcast is
  read-only" rule, easy to explain, is gone.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Alternatives:
  - Keep the rule and change only the owner's config to `default = "claude"`: no code, but
    then a plain message reaches only Claude.
  - Every recipient writable in its own worktree, changes returned as patches to apply:
    real multi-writer, but needs a merge step; can build on 0016 later.
  - Several writers in the checkout, serialized by the lock: they edit the same files
    from different plans, one after another; the conflict problem 0004 exists to avoid.
